import{_ as n,d as a,e as o,x as t}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"D.30 Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.30 Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living","xkChars":0,"xkWords":564},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-30.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-30.md"}'),i={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-30.md"};function s(r,e,c,l,d,u){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-30" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.30</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-30" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.30 Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living {#section-d-30}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §2.8, §5, §6.9, §12, and §14. Research entries cover 30.01–30.08.</p><p>Natural processes, institutional rules, knowledge, markets, and everyday life are connected through specific interfaces. Social choices can change resource use and the distribution of risks. They cannot remove hydrological or material constraints by definition. Resource systems, resource units, appropriators, rules in use, and outcomes call for domain-specific analysis. Forest cover, species, access to fuel, and household life cannot stand in for one another.</p><p>Distinguish food stocks, supplies an organization can requisition, and food households actually obtain (see Sen, 1981). In analyzing a food crisis, assess supply, access, delivery of relief, and consequences for nutrition and disease separately. Total stocks in storage or total imports alone do not establish that everyone’s needs have been met.</p><p>The costs of exclusion and the subtractability of common-pool resources are distinct from joint ownership under law. Consider rights of access, withdrawal, management, exclusion, and transfer separately. Collective organization and engineering support can complement one another. Local rules are not automatically fair. In energy transitions, distinguish continuous fuel flows, construction activity, in-use stocks, uses of minerals, grades of refined materials, and comparable services. Conservation accounting does not yield a unique allocation of value.</p><p>Water withdrawals, net consumption, evapotranspiration, return flows, recharge, and water levels are distinct. Teaching example. The cultivated area expands after field-level efficiency improves. Local water savings need not reduce total net consumption. Better information is also insufficient to guarantee the overall outcome if it does not feed into withdrawal constraints and maintenance practices. Distribution, recognition, procedure, and remedy in environmental justice each require their own evidence. Proximity to a pollution source does not by itself identify individual health effects.</p><p>In discussions of climate, distinguish historical contributions, current capabilities, future risks, and adaptation needs. Uncertainty about measurements, mechanisms, scenarios, and normative matters must also be kept separate. Support through cooperation, determinations of legal responsibility, and compensation for specific harms each depend on the applicable sources of law, objects, and conditions. Limits on one mechanism do not automatically rule out other grounds. Scientific assessments do not directly determine shares of compensation or authority to act. See International Court of Justice (2025, §§414–415, 433–435). GDP, material outcomes, living conditions, care, and ecological outcomes need not be combined. Conservation can concern individual nonhuman beings, populations, and ecological processes at the same time. Scientific knowledge, local knowledge, and legal representation involve distinct qualifications. Success in protecting one position does not automatically offset losses at another.</p><p>For ecological restoration, state the actual manipulation, the measured endpoint, and the target system separately. If the study directly changes fencing and hydrological infrastructure, the treatment cannot be renamed random reintroduction of predators. Even if only the combination reaches a given threshold, that does not by itself establish a statistically significant interaction. The number of species, biomass, water use, access to food, and people’s lives can change in different directions. Keeping the outcomes separate has more explanatory power than combining them into a health score without a basis.</p><p>Research topics. 30.01 Social–ecological systems and human–environment relations. 30.02 Commons, shared resources, and collective governance. 30.03 Energy transitions and material metabolism. 30.04 Agriculture, food, and water resources. 30.05 Environmental justice and the distribution of risk. 30.06 Climate change, adaptation, and intergenerational responsibility. 30.07 Growth, degrowth, and multiple scales of well-being. 30.08 Conservation, local communities, and nonhuman values.</p></div>',1)])])}const h=n(i,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,h as default};
